    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_transform_attrs`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_transform_attrs` function is a core part of the attrs library, responsible for transforming class attributes into a standardized form suitable for class creation. It takes several parameters: `cls` (the class being processed), `these` (a dictionary of attributes to override), `auto_attribs` (a flag to automatically convert annotated attributes to `attr.ib`), `kw_only` (a flag to make all attributes keyword-only), `collect_by_mro` (a flag to collect attributes by method resolution order), and `field_transformer` (a function to transform attributes before final processing). The function first gathers and validates attributes, then applies transformations, checks for mandatory attribute order, and finally creates a tuple class representing the attributes. It relies on helper functions like `_get_annotations`, `_collect_base_attrs`, and `_make_attr_tuple_class`. If unannotated attributes are found when `auto_attribs` is true, it raises an `UnannotatedAttributeError`. The function returns an `_Attributes` object containing the transformed attributes, base attributes, and a map of base attributes. It has no side effects beyond creating and returning the `_Attributes` object.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\